.TE
.SS Notices
.sp
Notices take these forms.
.SS note
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
A comment with additional information that explains a part of the
text.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS important
.sp
\fBImportant:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Something you must be aware of before proceeding.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS tip
.sp
\fBTip:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
An extra but helpful piece of practical advice.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS caution
.sp
\fBCaution:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Helpful information that prevents the user from making mistakes.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS seealso
.sp
\fBSee also:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
A reference to another piece of related information, like a
related setting or upstream documentation
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS warning
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Critical information about the risk of data loss or security
issues.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuration addition and deprecation notices
.sp
Added in version 9.0.0\-Mitaka.

.sp
Added in version 10.0.0\-Newton.

.sp
Changed in version 10.0.0\-Newton: Added support for new tags!

.sp
Changed in version 11.0.0\-Ocata: Showing that multiple notices work!

.sp
Deprecated since version 11.0.0\-Ocata: Use Notices instead.

.sp
Deprecated since version 12.0.0\-Pike: A second deprecation notice

.SS Configure access and security for instances
.sp
Before you launch an instance, you should add security group rules to
enable users to ping and use SSH to connect to the instance. Security
groups are sets of IP filter rules that define networking access and
are applied to all instances within a project. To do so, you either
add rules to the default security group \%<#\:add-a-rule-to-the-default-security-group> or add a new security
group with rules.
.sp
Key pairs are SSH credentials that are injected into an instance when it
is launched. To use key pair injection, the image that the instance is
based on must contain the \fBcloud\-init\fP package. Each project should
have at least one key pair. For more information, see import a key
pair \%<#\:import-a-key-pair>\&.
.sp
If you have generated a key pair with an external tool, you can import
it into OpenStack. The key pair can be used for multiple instances that
belong to a project. For more information, see ?  (TODO!).
.sp
When an instance is created in OpenStack, it is automatically assigned a
fixed IP address in the network to which the instance is assigned. This
IP address is permanently associated with the instance until the
instance is terminated. However, in addition to the fixed IP address, a
floating IP address can also be attached to an instance. Unlike fixed IP
addresses, floating IP addresses are able to have their associations
modified at any time, regardless of the state of the instances involved.
.SS Add a rule to the default security group
.sp
This procedure enables SSH and ICMP (ping) access to instances. The
rules apply to all instances within a given project, and should be set
for every project unless there is a reason to prohibit SSH or ICMP
access to the instances.
.sp
This procedure can be adjusted as necessary to add additional security
group rules to a project, if your cloud requires them.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When adding a rule, you must specify the protocol used with the
destination port or source port.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Access &
Security\&.  The Security Groups tab shows the security
groups that are available for this project.
.IP 2. 3
Select the default security group and click Edit Rules\&.
.IP 3. 3
To allow SSH access, click Add Rule\&.
.IP 4. 3
In the Add Rule dialog box, enter the following values:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Rule
T}	T{
\fBSSH\fP
T}
_
T{
Remote
T}	T{
\fBCIDR\fP
T}
_
T{
CIDR
T}	T{
\fB0.0.0.0/0\fP
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
To accept requests from a particular range of IP addresses, specify
the IP address block in the CIDR box.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 5. 3
Click Add\&.
.sp
Instances will now have SSH port 22 open for requests from any IP
address.
.IP 6. 3
To add an ICMP rule, click Add Rule\&.
.IP 7. 3
In the Add Rule dialog box, enter the following values:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Rule
T}	T{
\fBAll ICMP\fP
T}
_
T{
Direction
T}	T{
\fBIngress\fP
T}
_
T{
Remote
T}	T{
\fBCIDR\fP
T}
_
T{
CIDR
T}	T{
\fB0.0.0.0/0\fP
T}
.TE
.IP 8. 3
Click Add\&.
.sp
Instances will now accept all incoming ICMP packets.
.UNINDENT
.SS Add a key pair
.sp
Create at least one key pair for each project.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click
Access & Security\&.
.IP 2. 3
Click the Keypairs tab, which shows the key pairs
that are available for this project.
.IP 3. 3
Click Create Keypair\&.
.IP 4. 3
In the Create Keypair dialog box, enter a name for your
key pair, and click Create Keypair\&.
.IP 5. 3
Respond to the prompt to download the key pair.
.UNINDENT
.SS Import a key pair
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click
Access & Security\&.
.IP 2. 3
Click the Keypairs tab, which shows the key pairs that
are available for this project.
.IP 3. 3
Click Import Keypair\&.
.IP 4. 3
In the Import Keypair dialog box, enter the name of
your  key pair, copy the public key into the Public Key
box, and then click Import Keypair\&.
.IP 5. 3
Save the \fB*.pem\fP file locally.
.IP 6. 3
To change its permissions so that only you can read and write to the
file, run the following command:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ chmod 0600 yourPrivateKey.pem
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
If you are using the dashboard from a Windows computer, use PuTTYgen
to load the \fB*.pem\fP file and convert and save it as \fB*.ppk\fP\&. For
more information see the WinSCP web page for
PuTTYgen \%<http://\:winscp\:.net/\:eng/\:docs/\:ui_puttygen>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 7. 3
To make the key pair known to SSH, run the \fBssh\-add\fP command:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ssh\-add yourPrivateKey.pem
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Compute database registers the public key of the key pair.
.sp
The dashboard lists the key pair on the Access & Security tab.
.SS Allocate a floating IP address to an instance
.sp
When an instance is created in OpenStack, it is automatically assigned a
fixed IP address in the network to which the instance is assigned. This
IP address is permanently associated with the instance until the
instance is terminated.
.sp
However, in addition to the fixed IP address, a floating IP address can
also be attached to an instance. Unlike fixed IP addresses, floating IP
addresses can have their associations modified at any time, regardless
of the state of the instances involved. This procedure details the
reservation of a floating IP address from an existing pool of addresses
and the association of that address with a specific instance.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Access
& Security\&.
.IP 2. 3
Click the Floating IPs tab, which shows the floating IP
addresses allocated to instances.
.IP 3. 3
Click Allocate IP to Project\&.
.IP 4. 3
Choose the pool from which to pick the IP address.
.IP 5. 3
Click Allocate IP\&.
.IP 6. 3
In the Floating IPs list, click Associate\&.
.IP 7. 3
In the Manage Floating IP Associations dialog box,
choose the following options:
.INDENT 3.0
.IP \(bu 2
The IP Address field is filled automatically, but you
can add a new IP address by clicking the + button.
.IP \(bu 2
In the Ports to be associated field, select a port
from the list.
The list shows all the instances with their fixed
IP addresses.
.UNINDENT
.IP 8. 3
Click Associate\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
To disassociate an IP address from an instance, click the
Disassociate button.
.sp
To release the floating IP address back into the pool of
addresses, click the More button and select the
Release Floating IP option.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS OpenStack dashboard
.sp
As a cloud end user, you can use the OpenStack dashboard to provision
your own resources within the limits set by administrators. You can
modify the examples provided in this section to create other types and
sizes of server instances.
.SS Log in to the dashboard
.sp
The dashboard is available on the node with the \fBnova\-dashboard\fP
server role.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Ask the cloud operator for the host name or public IP address from
which you can access the dashboard, and for your user name and
password.
.IP 2. 3
Open a web browser that has JavaScript and cookies enabled.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
To use the Virtual Network Computing (VNC) client for the dashboard,
your browser must support HTML5 Canvas and HTML5 WebSockets. The VNC
client is based on noVNC. For details, see noVNC: HTML5 VNC
Client \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:kanaka/\:noVNC/\:blob/\:master/\:README\:.md>\&.
For a list of supported browsers, see Browser
support \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:kanaka/\:noVNC/\:wiki/\:Browser-support>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 3. 3
In the address bar, enter the host name or IP address for the
dashboard.
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
https://ipAddressOrHostName/
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
If a certificate warning appears when you try to access the URL for
the first time, a self\-signed certificate is in use, which is not
considered trustworthy by default. Verify the certificate or add an
exception in the browser to bypass the warning.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 4. 3
On the Log In page, enter your user name and password, and click Sign
In.
.sp
The top of the window displays your user name. You can also access
Settings or sign out of the dashboard.
.sp
The visible tabs and functions in the dashboard depend on the access
permissions, or roles, of the user you are logged in as.
.INDENT 3.0
.IP \(bu 2
If you are logged in as an end user, the Project tab is displayed.
.IP \(bu 2
If you are logged in as an administrator, the Project tab
(OpenStack dashboard—Project tab) and Admin tab
(OpenStack dashboard—Admin tab) are displayed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS OpenStack dashboard—Project tab
.sp
Projects are organizational units in the cloud, and are also known as
tenants or accounts. Each user is a member of one or more projects.
Within a project, a user creates and manages instances.
.sp
From the Project tab, you can view and manage the resources in a
selected project, including instances and images. You select the project
from the CURRENT PROJECT list at the top of the tab.
.sp
\fBFigure:\ Project tab\fP
[image]
.SS Project tab
.sp
From the Project tab, you can access the following tabs:
.SS Compute tab
.sp
Overview: View reports for the project.
.sp
Instances: View, launch, create a snapshot from, stop, pause, or
reboot instances, or connect to them through VNC.
.sp
Volumes: Use the following tabs to complete these tasks:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Volumes: View, create, edit, and delete volumes.
.IP \(bu 2
Volume Snapshots: View, create, edit, and delete volume
snapshots.
.IP \(bu 2
Images: View images and instance snapshots created by project
users, plus any images that are publicly available. Create, edit, and delete
images, and launch instances from images and snapshots.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Access & Security: Use the following tabs to complete these tasks:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Security Groups: View, create, edit, and delete security groups
and security group rules.
.IP \(bu 2
Key Pairs: View, create, edit, import, and delete key pairs.
.IP \(bu 2
Floating IPs: Allocate an IP address to or release it from a
project.
.IP \(bu 2
API Access: View API endpoints.
.UNINDENT
.SS Network tab
.sp
Network Topology: View the network topology.
.sp
Networks: Create and manage public and private networks.
.sp
Routers: Create and manage subnets.
.SS Object Store tab
.sp
Containers: Create and manage containers and objects.
.SS Orchestration tab
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Stacks: Use the REST API to orchestrate multiple composite cloud
applications.
.UNINDENT
.SS OpenStack dashboard—Admin tab
.sp
Administrative users can use the Admin tab to view usage and to
manage instances, volumes, flavors, images, projects, users, services, and
quotas.
.sp
\fBFigure:\ Admin tab\fP
[image]
.SS Admin tab
.sp
Access the following categories to complete these tasks:
.SS System Panel tab
.sp
Overview: View basic reports.
.sp
Resource Usage: Use the following tabs to view the following usages:
.sp
Daily Report: View the daily report.
.sp
Stats: View the statistics of all resources.
.sp
Hypervisors: View the hypervisor summary.
.sp
Host Aggregates: View, create, and edit host aggregates. View the
list of availability zones.
.sp
Instances: View, pause, resume, suspend, migrate, soft or hard
reboot, and delete running instances that belong to users of some, but not all,
projects. Also, view the log for an instance or access an instance through VNC.
.sp
Volumes: View, create, edit, and delete volumes and volume types.
.sp
Flavors: View, create, edit, view extra specifications for, and
delete flavors. A flavor is size of an instance.
.sp
Images: View, create, edit properties for, and delete custom
images.
.sp
Networks: View, create, edit properties for, and delete networks.
.sp
Routers: View, create, edit properties for, and delete routers.
.sp
System Info: Use the following tabs to view the service
information:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Services: View a list of the services.
.IP \(bu 2
Compute Services: View a list of all Compute services.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Network Agents: View the network agents.
.sp
Default Quotas: View default quota values. Quotas are hard\-coded in
OpenStack Compute and define the maximum allowable size and number of
resources.
.SS Identity Panel tab
.sp
Projects: View, create, assign users to, remove users from, and
delete projects.
.sp
Users: View, create, enable, disable, and delete users.
.SS Configure access and security for instances
.sp
Before you launch an instance, you should add security group rules to
enable users to ping and use SSH to connect to the instance. Security
groups are sets of IP filter rules that define networking access and are
applied to all instances within a project. To do so, you either add
rules to the default security group Add a rule to the default security group
or add a new security group with rules.
.sp
Key pairs are SSH credentials that are injected into an instance when it
is launched. To use key pair injection, the image that the instance is
based on must contain the \fBcloud\-init\fP package. Each project should
have at least one key pair. For more information, see the section
Add a key pair\&.
.sp
If you have generated a key pair with an external tool, you can import
it into OpenStack. The key pair can be used for multiple instances that
belong to a project. For more information, see the section
Import a key pair\&.
.sp
When an instance is created in OpenStack, it is automatically assigned a
fixed IP address in the network to which the instance is assigned. This
IP address is permanently associated with the instance until the
instance is terminated. However, in addition to the fixed IP address, a
floating IP address can also be attached to an instance. Unlike fixed IP
addresses, floating IP addresses are able to have their associations
modified at any time, regardless of the state of the instances involved.
.SS Add a rule to the default security group
.sp
This procedure enables SSH and ICMP (ping) access to instances. The
rules apply to all instances within a given project, and should be set
for every project unless there is a reason to prohibit SSH or ICMP
access to the instances.
.sp
This procedure can be adjusted as necessary to add additional security
group rules to a project, if your cloud requires them.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When adding a rule, you must specify the protocol used with the
destination port or source port.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Access &
Security\&. The Security Groups tab shows the security groups
that are available for this project.
.IP 2. 3
Select the default security group and click Edit Rules\&.
.IP 3. 3
To allow SSH access, click Add Rule\&.
.IP 4. 3
In the Add Rule dialog box, enter the following values:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Rule
T}	T{
Remote
T}
_
T{
\fBSSH\fP
T}	T{
\fBCIDR\fP
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
To accept requests from a particular range of IP addresses, specify
the IP address block in the CIDR box.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 5. 3
Click Add\&.
.sp
Instances will now have SSH port 22 open for requests from any IP
address.
.IP 6. 3
To add an ICMP rule, click Add Rule\&.
.IP 7. 3
In the Add Rule dialog box, enter the following values:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Rule
T}	T{
Direction
T}
_
T{
\fBAll ICMP\fP
T}	T{
\fBIngress\fP
T}
.TE
.IP 8. 3
Click Add\&.
.sp
Instances will now accept all incoming ICMP packets.
.UNINDENT
.SS Add a key pair
.sp
Create at least one key pair for each project.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Access &
Security.
.IP 2. 3
Click the Keypairs tab, which shows the key pairs that are available
for this project.
.IP 3. 3
Click Create Keypair.
.IP 4. 3
In the Create Keypair dialog box, enter a name for your key pair, and
click Create Keypair.
.IP 5. 3
Respond to the prompt to download the key pair.
.UNINDENT
.SS Import a key pair
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Access &
Security.
.IP 2. 3
Click the Keypairs tab, which shows the key pairs that are available
for this project.
.IP 3. 3
Click Import Keypair.
.IP 4. 3
In the Import Keypair dialog box, enter the name of your key pair,
copy the public key into the Public Key box, and then click Import
Keypair.
.IP 5. 3
Save the \fB*.pem\fP file locally.
.IP 6. 3
To change its permissions so that only you can read and write to the
file, run the following command:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ chmod 0600 yourPrivateKey.pem
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
If you are using the dashboard from a Windows computer, use PuTTYgen
to load the \fB*.pem\fP file and convert and save it as \fB*.ppk\fP\&. For
more information see the WinSCP web page for
PuTTYgen \%<http://\:winscp\:.net/\:eng/\:docs/\:ui-puttygen>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 7. 3
To make the key pair known to SSH, run the \fBssh\-add\fP command.
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ssh\-add yourPrivateKey.pem
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Compute database registers the public key of the key pair.
.sp
The dashboard lists the key pair on the Access & Security tab.
.SS Allocate a floating IP address to an instance
.sp
When an instance is created in OpenStack, it is automatically assigned a
fixed IP address in the network to which the instance is assigned. This
IP address is permanently associated with the instance until the
instance is terminated.
.sp
However, in addition to the fixed IP address, a floating IP address can
also be attached to an instance. Unlike fixed IP addresses, floating IP
addresses can have their associations modified at any time, regardless
of the state of the instances involved. This procedure details the
reservation of a floating IP address from an existing pool of addresses
and the association of that address with a specific instance.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Access &
Security.
.IP 2. 3
Click the Floating IPs tab, which shows the floating IP addresses
allocated to instances.
.IP 3. 3
Click Allocate IP to Project.
.IP 4. 3
Choose the pool from which to pick the IP address.
.IP 5. 3
Click Allocate IP.
.IP 6. 3
In the Floating IPs list, click Associate.
.IP 7. 3
In the Manage Floating IP Associations dialog box, choose the
following options:
.INDENT 3.0
.IP 1. 3
The IP Address field is filled automatically, but you can add a
new IP address by clicking the + button.
.IP 2. 3
In the Ports to be associated field, select a port from the list.
.sp
The list shows all the instances with their fixed IP addresses.
.UNINDENT
.IP 8. 3
Click Associate.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
To disassociate an IP address from an instance, click the
Disassociate button.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To release the floating IP address back into the pool of addresses,
click the More button and select the Release
Floating IP option.
.SS Create and manage databases
.sp
The Database service provides scalable and reliable cloud provisioning
functionality for both relational and non\-relational database engines.
Users can quickly and easily use database features without the burden of
handling complex administrative tasks.
.SS Create a database instance
.sp
\fBPrerequisites.\fP Before you create a database instance, you need to
configure a default datastore and make sure you have an appropriate
flavor for the type of database instance you want.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
\fBConfigure a default datastore.\fP
.sp
Because the dashboard does not let you choose a specific datastore to
use with an instance, you need to configure a default datastore. The
dashboard then uses the default datastore to create the instance.
.INDENT 3.0
.IP 1. 3
Add the following line to \fB/etc/trove/trove.conf\fP:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
default_datastore = DATASTORE_NAME
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Replace \fI\(ga\(gaDATASTORE_NAME\(ga\(ga\fP with the name that the administrative
user set when issuing the \fBtrove\-manage\fP command to create the
datastore. You can use the trove \fBdatastore\-list\fP command to
display the datastores that are available in your environment.
.sp
For example, if your MySQL datastore name is set to \fBmysql\fP,
your entry would look like this:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
default_datastore = mysql
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 2. 3
Restart Database services on the controller node:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# service trove\-api restart
# service trove\-taskmanager restart
# service trove\-conductor restart
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 2. 3
\fBVerify flavor.\fP
.sp
Make sure an appropriate flavor exists for the type of
database instance you want.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBCreate database instance.\fP Once you have configured a default
datastore and verified that you have an appropriate flavor, you can
create a database instance.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Databases\&.
.IP 2. 3
Click Database Instances\&. This lists the instances that already
exist in your environment.
.IP 3. 3
Click Launch Instance\&.
.IP 4. 3
In the Launch Database dialog box, specify the following values.
.sp
Details
.sp
Database Name: Specify a name for the database instance.
.sp
Flavor: Select an appropriate flavor for the instance.
.sp
Volume Size: Select a volume size. Volume size is expressed in
GB.
.sp
Initialize Databases: Initial Database
.sp
Optionally provide a comma separated list of databases to create, for
example:
.sp
\fBdatabase1\fP, \fBdatabase2\fP, \fBdatabase3\fP
.sp
Initial Admin User: Create an initial admin user. This user will
have access to all the databases you create.
.sp
Password: Specify a password associated with the initial admin
user you just named.
.sp
Host: Optionally, allow the user to connect only from this host.
If you do not specify a host, this user will be allowed to connect from
anywhere.
.IP 5. 3
Click the Launch button\&. The new database instance appears in the
databases list.
.UNINDENT
.SS Backup and restore a database
.sp
You can use Database services to backup a database and store the backup
artifact in the Object Storage module. Later on, if the original
database is damaged, you can use the backup artifact to restore the
database. The restore process creates a database instance.
.sp
This example shows you how to back up and restore a MySQL database.
.SS To backup the database instance
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click
Databases\&.
.IP 2. 3
Click Database Instances\&. This displays the existing
instances in your system.
.IP 3. 3
Click Create Backup\&.
.IP 4. 3
In the Backup Database dialog box, specify the following
values:
.sp
Name
.sp
Specify a name for the backup.
.sp
Database Instance
.sp
Select the instance you want to back up.
.IP 5. 3
Click Backup. The new backup appears in the backup list.
.UNINDENT
.SS To restore a database instance
.sp
Now assume that your original database instance is damaged and you
need to restore it. You do the restore by using your backup to create
a new database instance.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click
Databases\&.
.IP 2. 3
Click Database Backups\&. This lists the available backups.
.IP 3. 3
Check the backup you want to use and click Restore Backup\&.
.IP 4. 3
In the Launch Database dialog box, specify the values you
want for the new database instance.
.IP 5. 3
Click the Restore From Database tab and make sure that this
new instance is based on the correct backup.
.IP 6. 3
Click Launch\&.
.sp
The new instance appears in the database instances list.
.UNINDENT
.SS Create and manage networks
.sp
The OpenStack Networking service provides a scalable system for managing
the network connectivity within an OpenStack cloud deployment. It can
easily and quickly react to changing network needs (for example,
creating and assigning new IP addresses).
.sp
Networking in OpenStack is complex. This section provides the basic
instructions for creating a network and a router. For detailed
information about managing networks, refer to the OpenStack
Administrator
Guide \%<https://\:docs\:.openstack\:.org/\:neutron/\:latest/\:admin>\&.
.SS Create a network
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Networks.
.IP 2. 3
Click Create Network.
.IP 3. 3
In the Create Network dialog box, specify the following values.
.sp
Network tab
.sp
Network Name: Specify a name to identify the network.
.sp
Subnet tab
.sp
Create Subnet: Select this check box to create a subnet
.sp
You do not have to specify a subnet when you create a network, but if
you do not specify a subnet, any attached instance receives an Error status.
.sp
Subnet Name: Specify a name for the subnet.
.sp
Network Address: Specify the IP address for the subnet.
.sp
IP Version: Select IPv4 or IPv6.
.sp
Gateway IP: Specify an IP address for a specific gateway. This
parameter is optional.
.sp
Disable Gateway: Select this check box to disable a gateway IP
address.
.sp
Subnet Detail tab
.sp
Enable DHCP: Select this check box to enable DHCP.
.sp
Allocation Pools Specify IP address pools.
.sp
DNS Name Servers: Specify a name for the DNS server.
.sp
Host Routes: Specify the IP address of host routes.
.IP 4. 3
Click Create\&.
.sp
The dashboard shows the network on the Networks tab.
.UNINDENT
.SS Create a router
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log in to the dashboard, choose a project, and click Routers\&.
.IP 2. 3
Click Create Router\&.
.IP 3. 3
In the Create Router dialog box, specify a name for the router
and click Create Router\&.
.sp
The new router is now displayed in the Routers tab.
.IP 4. 3
Click the new router\(aqs Set Gateway button.
.IP 5. 3
In the External Network field, specify the network to which the
router will connect, and then click Set Gateway\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS Connect a router
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
To connect a private network to the newly created router, on the
Routers tab, click the name of the router.
.IP 2. 3
On the Router Details page, click Add Interface\&.
.IP 3. 3
In the Add Interface dialog box, select a Subnet\&.
.IP 4. 3
Optionally, in the Add Interface dialog box, set an
IP Address for the router interface for the selected subnet.
.sp
If you choose not to set the IP Address value, then by
default OpenStack Networking uses the first host IP address in the subnet.
.sp
The Router Name and Router ID fields are
automatically updated.
.IP 5. 3
Click Add Interface\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
You have successfully created the router. You can view the new topology
from the Network Topology tab.
.SS Launch an instance
.SS To access your instance remotely
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Add rules to the \fBdefault\fP security group:
.INDENT 3.0
.IP a. 3
Permit \fBICMP\fP (ping):
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ nova secgroup\-add\-rule default icmp \-1 \-1 0.0.0.0/0
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| IP Protocol | From Port | To Port | IP Range  | Source Group |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| icmp        | \-1        | \-1      | 0.0.0.0/0 |              |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP b. 3
Permit secure shell (SSH) access:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ nova secgroup\-add\-rule default tcp 22 22 0.0.0.0/0
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| IP Protocol | From Port | To Port | IP Range  | Source Group |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| tcp         | 22        | 22      | 0.0.0.0/0 |              |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 2. 3
Create a \fBfloating IP address\fP on the \fBext\-net\fP external network:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ neutron floatingip\-create ext\-net
Created a new floatingip:
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| Field               | Value                                |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| fixed_ip_address    |                                      |
| floating_ip_address | 203.0.113.102                        |
| floating_network_id | 9bce64a3\-a963\-4c05\-bfcd\-161f708042d1 |
| id                  | 05e36754\-e7f3\-46bb\-9eaa\-3521623b3722 |
| port_id             |                                      |
| router_id           |                                      |
| status              | DOWN                                 |
| tenant_id           | 7cf50047f8df4824bc76c2fdf66d11ec     |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Testing ordered lists and auto\-numbering
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Item 1
.INDENT 3.0
.IP 1. 3
Item 1, Item 1
.IP 2. 3
Item 2, Item 1
.UNINDENT
.IP 2. 3
